1
00:04:57,142 --> 00:05:03,142
If these walls could talk... they would shriek!

2
00:09:54,285 --> 00:10:00,285
Amityville II: The Possession is a horror film directed by Damiano Damiani.

3
00:14:51,428 --> 00:14:57,428
Anthony and Dolores Montelli, along with their four children, move into their dream house in
Amityville and are immediately plagued by a string of paranormal experiences.

4
00:19:48,571 --> 00:19:54,571
When the abusive Anthony wrongly places the blame on their children,
Dolores recruits a local Catholic priest to exorcise the house.

5
00:24:45,714 --> 00:24:51,714
Father Adamsky, played by James Olson.

6
00:29:42,857 --> 00:29:48,857
Anthony Montelli, played by Burt Young.

7
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:46,000
Delores Montelli, played by Rutanya Alda.

8
00:39:37,142 --> 00:39:43,142
Sonny Montelli, played by Jack Magner.

9
00:44:34,285 --> 00:44:40,285
Father Tom, played by Andrew Prine.

10
00:49:31,428 --> 00:49:37,428
Patricia Montelli, played by Diane Franklin.

11
00:54:28,571 --> 00:54:34,571
Amityville II: The Possession is a 1982 supernatural horror film directed by Damiano Damiani and
starring James Olson, Burt Young, Rutanya Alda, Jack Magner, and Diane Franklin.

12
00:59:25,714 --> 00:59:31,714
It is an international co-production between Mexico and the United States.

13
01:04:22,857 --> 01:04:28,857
The screenplay by Tommy Lee Wallace and Dardano Sacchetti is based on the
novel Murder in Amityville by the parapsychologist Hans Holzer.

14
01:09:20,000 --> 01:09:26,000
It is the second film in the Amityville Horror film series and a loose prequel to The Amityville Horror (1979), set
at 112 Ocean Avenue and featuring the fictional Montelli family, loosely based on the DeFeo family.

15
01:14:17,142 --> 01:14:23,142
It follows the Montelli family's decline under apparent
demonic forces present in their home.

16
01:19:14,285 --> 01:19:20,285
Principal photography took place at the same Toms River, New Jersey residence featured in the first film,
while interiors were shot exclusively on soundstages at Estudios Churubusco in Mexico City.

17
01:24:11,428 --> 01:24:17,428
After test screenings were completed, Damiani's original cut of the film was slightly truncated to tone down
its overt incestuous sexuality and a rape sequence, which audiences responded to unfavorably.

18
01:29:08,571 --> 01:29:14,571
Released by Orion Pictures in the fall of 1982, Amityville II: The Possession received unfavorable reviews from critics, though some,
such as Roger Ebert, felt it was superior to its predecessor, an opinion that has been echoed by contemporary reviewers.

19
01:34:05,714 --> 01:34:11,714
The film explores themes of child abuse, sibling
relationship, abusive father, exorcism, poltergeist.

20
01:39:02,857 --> 01:39:08,857
The tone is lighthearted and dark.
